# hde-keyring — the two lines that make the password you type at the login screen open the keyring as well.
#
#   sudo cp packaging/pam/hde-keyring /usr/share/doc/...      `make install` puts it in $(PREFIX)/share/hde/pam/
#
# Add them to the PAM file of the display manager: /etc/pam.d/sddm on a machine using SDDM (HDE's own login
# screen), /etc/pam.d/gdm-password for GDM, /etc/pam.d/lightdm for LightDM. The first line belongs with the other
# "auth" lines, the second with the other "session" lines — PAM runs them in the order of the file, and
# pam_gnome_keyring.so only keeps the password if it is asked after the password has been checked.
#
#   auth       optional   pam_gnome_keyring.so             remembers the password that was just checked
#   session    optional   pam_gnome_keyring.so auto_start  starts the daemon and unlocks the keyring with it
#
# Without these lines HDE still has a keyring: hde-session starts gnome-keyring-daemon before anything else, so
# the browser, Git and NetworkManager all find the one Secret Service and keep their passwords in it. It just
# starts out locked, and the first program that needs a password has to ask for it again.
#
# The password itself never reaches the session: it goes from PAM to the daemon, and only the socket of the
# unlocked keyring comes back (as GNOME_KEYRING_CONTROL / SSH_AUTH_SOCK, which hde-session exports).

auth       optional     pam_gnome_keyring.so
session    optional     pam_gnome_keyring.so auto_start
